# Product Requirements Document (PRD)

## Sovereign On-Premise Agentic AI Workbench
**A provably air-gapped, privacy-preserving AI agent platform**

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Hackathon | IndustrySolve Hackathon, IIIT Delhi 2026 |
| Theme | 6. Cybersecurity & Privacy-Preserving Technology |
| Origin problem | MRPL PS 26117 (Smart Automation, Software), used as the primary use-case domain |
| Document status | Draft v1.0 |
| Related doc | `sovereign-agentic-ai-workbench-architecture.md` |

---

## 1. Overview

### 1.1 One-line pitch
An on-premise AI agent platform that lets organisations use powerful open-weight AI on sensitive data with **cryptographic and network-level proof** that nothing leaves the building, and with **built-in consent, access control and erasure** for the data the AI touches.

### 1.2 Problem statement
Organisations that handle sensitive data (refineries, PSUs, defence-linked firms, hospitals, banks) cannot safely use cloud AI tools. Sending documents, drawings, code or personal records to a third-party API creates leakage, compliance and sovereignty risks. Existing local-LLM setups (e.g. Ollama + a chat UI) solve only the "run a model locally" part. They do not provide:

- Proof that no data egresses (claims are made, not demonstrated).
- Safe execution of AI-generated code and tool calls.
- Access-controlled retrieval over internal documents.
- Consent, purpose limitation and erasure for personal data processed by the AI.
- An auditable record of what the AI did and why.

### 1.3 Why this fits Theme 6
Theme 6 asks for systems that **resist compromise** and **reduce unnecessary collection of personal data**. This product addresses both:

| Theme 6 requirement | How the product addresses it |
|---|---|
| Resist compromise | Zero-egress network design, sandboxed tool execution, signed model weights, tamper-evident audit ledger |
| Protect privacy | Access-scoped retrieval, PII redaction, purpose-limited data use |
| Consent dashboard / erasure | Governance dashboard to view data usage, withdraw consent per purpose, and trigger erasure |
| Reduced data collection | Data minimisation by design: no external calls, no telemetry, only task-relevant chunks enter model context |

---

## 2. Goals and Non-Goals

### 2.1 Goals
1. **Provable sovereignty:** demonstrate zero outbound connections during a full agent session.
2. **Capable agentic AI:** multi-step tasks (plan, execute, verify, deliver) running entirely on local models.
3. **Model-agnostic:** new open-weight models can be added through configuration only.
4. **Privacy by design:** RBAC-scoped retrieval, PII redaction, consent and erasure controls.
5. **Auditability:** every model call, tool call and file access is recorded in a verifiable log.
6. **Hackathon-demo ready:** the full system runs on a single mid-range GPU (24-48 GB VRAM).

### 2.2 Non-Goals
- Training or fine-tuning foundation models.
- Competing with frontier cloud models on raw capability.
- Replacing enterprise IAM; the product integrates with existing LDAP/Keycloak.
- Real-time control of industrial equipment (the system advises and drafts; humans decide).
- Certified compliance (e.g. ISO 27001) within the hackathon scope; the design supports it but certification is out of scope.

---

## 3. Target Users and Personas

| Persona | Description | Key needs |
|---|---|---|
| **Engineer / Analyst** (primary) | Reviews inspection reports, drawings and datasets; writes calculations and scripts | Fast, accurate drafting; trustworthy citations; no data leaving the network |
| **Manager / Approver** | Reviews and signs off on notes and presentations | Clear summaries, visible sources, explicit approval gates |
| **Security / IT Administrator** | Deploys and governs the system | Proof of isolation, model management, audit access, update control |
| **Data Protection / Compliance Officer** | Owns privacy obligations | Consent view, erasure workflow, access logs, classification enforcement |

---

## 4. Use Cases

1. **Inspection report to approval note.** Upload a scanned inspection report; the agent extracts findings via OCR/VLM, grounds them with SOPs from the knowledge base, and drafts a `.docx` approval note with citations.
2. **Engineering calculation.** The agent writes and runs code in a sandbox to compute results and delivers an `.xlsx` with live formulas for audit.
3. **Drawing / P&ID understanding.** Extract equipment tags and annotations with confidence scores; low-confidence fields are flagged for human review.
4. **Knowledge-base Q&A.** Ask questions over internal documents with RBAC-filtered, cited answers.
5. **Governance.** A compliance officer views which documents were used by which sessions, withdraws consent for a purpose, and triggers erasure.
6. **Sovereignty verification.** An administrator opens the live dashboard and audit ledger to verify zero egress and log integrity.

---

## 5. Functional Requirements

Priority key: **P0** = must have for demo, **P1** = should have, **P2** = stretch.

### 5.1 Model Serving and Routing
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-1.1 | Serve multiple open-weight models locally through a pluggable backend (vLLM for production; Ollama acceptable for demo) | P0 |
| FR-1.2 | Declarative YAML model registry; adding a model requires no code change | P0 |
| FR-1.3 | Two-stage router: intent classifier (task type, modality, complexity, sensitivity) followed by capability-matched model selection | P0 |
| FR-1.4 | Routing decision visible in the UI for every task | P0 |
| FR-1.5 | Fallback chain when a preferred model is unavailable or out of memory | P1 |
| FR-1.6 | LRU model swapping to run multiple models on one GPU | P1 |
| FR-1.7 | Optional verifier pass: a second model checks high-stakes outputs | P2 |
| FR-1.8 | Runtime model reload via admin endpoint without redeploy | P1 |

### 5.2 Agent Orchestration
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-2.1 | Planner decomposes tasks into a DAG of sub-tasks | P0 |
| FR-2.2 | Each sub-task cycle: route, execute, observe, critique, then retry (max 3) or complete | P0 |
| FR-2.3 | Explicit, inspectable JSON task state shown as a live "agent trace" panel | P0 |
| FR-2.4 | Human-in-the-loop checkpoint gates, configurable per task type | P0 |
| FR-2.5 | Bounded autonomy: configurable max depth, retries, tool calls and wall-clock timeout | P1 |
| FR-2.6 | Session persistence so long tasks can be paused and resumed | P2 |

### 5.3 Tool Execution Sandbox
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-3.1 | Code runs in an ephemeral isolated container (gVisor/Firecracker or hardened Docker) with **no network namespace** | P0 |
| FR-3.2 | File access only through a broker enforcing path allowlist and user RBAC | P0 |
| FR-3.3 | seccomp filter blocking non-loopback `connect()` as defense in depth | P1 |
| FR-3.4 | Execution logs (stdout, stderr, test results) returned with every code deliverable | P0 |
| FR-3.5 | Tools registered via OpenAI-compatible function-calling schema (model-agnostic) | P1 |

### 5.4 Knowledge Base and Retrieval (RAG)
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-4.1 | Ingest PDF (text and scanned), DOCX, XLSX and email formats | P0 |
| FR-4.2 | Table-aware chunking; on-prem multilingual embeddings (e.g. BGE-M3) | P1 |
| FR-4.3 | Self-hosted vector store (Qdrant/Milvus), encrypted at rest | P0 |
| FR-4.4 | Hybrid retrieval (dense + BM25 + metadata filters) with local re-ranking | P1 |
| FR-4.5 | **RBAC and classification filtering applied before chunks reach the model context** | P0 |
| FR-4.6 | Mandatory citations (document, page/section, last-modified); answer "not found" when confidence is low | P0 |
| FR-4.7 | Incremental re-indexing on file changes | P2 |

### 5.5 Multimodal Pipeline
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-5.1 | Pre-processing (deskew, denoise, contrast) for scanned input | P1 |
| FR-5.2 | Printed-text OCR (PaddleOCR/Tesseract) with bounding boxes | P0 |
| FR-5.3 | Handwriting and diagram understanding via a vision-language model | P1 |
| FR-5.4 | Structured JSON output with per-field confidence and source region | P0 |
| FR-5.5 | Fields below the confidence threshold are flagged for mandatory human confirmation | P0 |

### 5.6 Deliverable Generation
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-6.1 | `.docx` approval notes and reports from structured templates with citations | P0 |
| FR-6.2 | `.xlsx` outputs containing real formulas, not pasted values | P1 |
| FR-6.3 | `.pptx` presentations from an approved template | P2 |
| FR-6.4 | Code delivered as a diff with execution log | P1 |
| FR-6.5 | Every deliverable links to model version, prompt, retrieved chunks and tool calls | P1 |

### 5.6b Privacy and Consent Governance (Theme 6 core)
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-7.1 | **Consent and data-usage dashboard:** shows, per document/dataset, which sessions and purposes accessed it | P0 |
| FR-7.2 | **Consent withdrawal per purpose:** revoked purposes are blocked from retrieval immediately | P1 |
| FR-7.3 | **Erasure workflow:** deletes a document from the source store, vector index, caches and derived chunks, and records the erasure in the audit ledger | P1 |
| FR-7.4 | **Purpose-limited retrieval:** each session declares a purpose; only data tagged for that purpose is retrievable | P1 |
| FR-7.5 | **PII redaction** (local NER) before indexing, with measurable detection recall on a test set | P0 |
| FR-7.6 | Classification levels (Public / Internal / Confidential / Board-only) inherited by all outputs | P0 |
| FR-7.7 | Privacy-preserving aggregate query returning population-level statistics without exposing individual records | P2 |

### 5.7 Sovereignty Proof and Audit
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-8.1 | Stack runs with no default route to WAN; egress firewall defaults to DROP | P0 |
| FR-8.2 | Live egress monitor on a Grafana dashboard showing attempted outbound connections and DNS lookups | P0 |
| FR-8.3 | Append-only, hash-chained audit ledger for model calls, tool calls and file access, with a verification command | P0 |
| FR-8.4 | SHA-256 verification of model weights against a signed manifest at boot | P1 |
| FR-8.5 | Air-gapped update path via vetted offline transfer with checksum verification | P2 |
| FR-8.6 | Physical NIC-disconnect demo: system continues working identically | P0 |

### 5.8 Security Demonstrations (Threat Model)
| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| FR-9.1 | Documented threat model (see Section 8) | P0 |
| FR-9.2 | Demo: sandbox blocks a simulated exfiltration attempt from AI-generated code | P0 |
| FR-9.3 | Demo: prompt injection embedded in an uploaded document does not trigger unauthorised tool use or data access | P1 |
| FR-9.4 | Demo: tampered model weights or modified audit log entries are detected | P1 |

---

## 6. Non-Functional Requirements

| Category | Requirement |
|---|---|
| **Security** | No outbound network calls from any component; secrets encrypted; least-privilege service accounts |
| **Privacy** | No telemetry; data never used to train models; PII handled per classification |
| **Performance** | Interactive responses stream within seconds; simple tasks routed to small models for low latency |
| **Hardware** | Demo runs on a single GPU (24-48 GB); design scales to multi-GPU clusters |
| **Reliability** | Fallback models; bounded agent loops to prevent runaway compute |
| **Auditability** | 100% of model/tool/file actions logged; log integrity verifiable |
| **Extensibility** | New models, tools and routing rules added by configuration |
| **Usability** | Web UI with agent trace, citations and approval prompts understandable to non-technical staff |
| **Portability** | Docker Compose for demo; Kubernetes for production |

---

## 7. System Architecture (Summary)

Full detail is in the architecture document. Components:

1. **Client:** Web UI (chat, agent trace, approval prompts, governance dashboard).
2. **API layer:** FastAPI + WebSocket streaming; Keycloak/LDAP SSO with RBAC.
3. **Agent orchestration core:** planner, model router, tool dispatcher, memory manager, critic, checkpoint gate.
4. **Model serving layer:** vLLM (or Ollama for demo) with YAML model registry.
5. **Tool sandbox:** network-less ephemeral containers plus file broker.
6. **RAG engine:** parsers, embeddings, vector store, hybrid retriever with RBAC filter.
7. **Multimodal pipeline:** OCR and VLM with confidence gating.
8. **Governance layer:** consent, purpose limitation, erasure, PII redaction.
9. **Sovereignty and audit layer:** egress monitor, hash-chained ledger, weight verification.

Reference stack: vLLM/Ollama, Qwen3 / Qwen2.5-Coder / Qwen2.5-VL (GPT-OSS-120B where hardware permits), Qdrant, BGE-M3, PaddleOCR, FastAPI, Keycloak, Prometheus + Grafana, Docker/Kubernetes.

---

## 8. Threat Model

| # | Threat | Mitigation | Verified by |
|---|---|---|---|
| T1 | Data exfiltration to the internet by any component | No WAN route, DROP-all egress, egress monitor | Live dashboard, NIC unplug demo |
| T2 | AI-generated code attempts network calls or reads outside its workspace | Network-less sandbox, seccomp, file broker | Exfiltration attempt demo (FR-9.2) |
| T3 | Prompt injection via uploaded documents | Tool-call allowlist, human checkpoints, RBAC enforced outside the model | Injection demo (FR-9.3) |
| T4 | Unauthorised user reads documents above their clearance | Pre-retrieval RBAC and classification filter | Access test with two users |
| T5 | Tampered or substituted model weights | Signed manifest and SHA-256 check at boot | Tamper demo (FR-9.4) |
| T6 | Audit log manipulation | Hash-chained append-only ledger | Verification command detects edits |
| T7 | Personal data retained after consent withdrawal | Consent revocation and erasure workflow | Erasure demo (FR-7.3) |
| T8 | Hallucinated or wrong outputs used in decisions | Citations, confidence gating, critic pass, human sign-off | Low-confidence flagging demo |
| T9 | Malicious update via supply chain | Offline vetted updates with checksum verification | Process documentation |

**Out of scope threats:** physical compromise of the host, insider with root access, side-channel attacks on GPUs.

---

## 9. Success Metrics

| Metric | Target |
|---|---|
| External connections observed during full demo | **0** |
| Tasks auto-routed to a suitable model in the demo | ≥ 2 task types, correct model each time |
| End-to-end agentic task (scan to `.docx`) | Completed with citations |
| Sandbox exfiltration attempts blocked | 100% in the test set |
| PII detection recall on the test set | ≥ 90% (measured and reported) |
| Audit ledger tampering detected | 100% of test edits |
| Unauthorised retrieval across clearance levels | 0 leaks in the access test |
| Erasure completeness | Document absent from store, index and cache after erasure |

---

## 10. Demo Plan (5 steps)

1. **Auto-routing:** run a coding prompt and a summarisation prompt; the trace panel shows different models selected.
2. **End-to-end task:** scanned inspection report, then OCR/VLM extraction, then findings, then approval note `.docx` with citations.
3. **Sandboxed code:** the agent writes and runs a script; show isolation and execution logs. Then run the simulated exfiltration attempt and show it blocked.
4. **Privacy governance:** show the consent dashboard, withdraw consent for a purpose, trigger erasure, and show the ledger entry.
5. **Sovereignty proof:** live egress dashboard shows zero connections; unplug the NIC; verify the audit ledger hash chain.

---

## 11. Differentiation

**"Why not just Ollama + Open WebUI?"** Ollama is a local model runner, equivalent to one layer of this system. It does not provide capability-based routing, agent orchestration, sandboxed execution, RBAC-scoped retrieval, consent and erasure controls, or verifiable proof of zero egress. This product is the governed, auditable platform built on top of local models.

Key differentiators:
1. Provable (not claimed) sovereignty through monitoring and a tamper-evident ledger.
2. Sandboxed agent execution with defense in depth.
3. Privacy governance (consent, purpose limitation, erasure) as a first-class feature.
4. Capability-contract model registry and router.

---

## 12. Scope and Milestones

### 12.1 MVP (hackathon demo)
All **P0** requirements: routing across at least two models, agent trace, sandboxed code, RAG with RBAC filter and citations, OCR extraction with confidence flags, `.docx` generation, consent dashboard (view), PII redaction, egress monitor, hash-chained ledger, threat model and exfiltration demo.

### 12.2 Post-MVP
**P1** items (consent withdrawal, erasure workflow, purpose limitation, weight verification, fallback chain, `.xlsx` formulas) followed by **P2** (verifier pass, `.pptx`, aggregate privacy-preserving queries, session resume, air-gapped update path).

### 12.3 Suggested timeline (adjust to hackathon length)
| Phase | Focus |
|---|---|
| 1 | Model serving, registry, router, API skeleton |
| 2 | Agent core and sandbox |
| 3 | RAG with RBAC, OCR/VLM pipeline |
| 4 | Governance (PII, consent, erasure), audit ledger, egress monitor |
| 5 | Deliverable generation, UI polish, threat-model demos, rehearsal |

---

## 13. Risks and Mitigations

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Limited GPU memory at the venue | Models fail to load | Quantised models, LRU swapping, fallback chain, smallest-model default |
| Scope too large for the hackathon | Incomplete demo | Strict P0/P1/P2 prioritisation; build the sovereignty and governance demos early |
| Local model quality below expectations | Weak outputs | Verifier pass, citations, human checkpoints, constrained task scope |
| Looks like "just Ollama" to judges | Weak differentiation | Lead with proof, sandbox and governance demos |
| Judges see security as a side feature | Poor Theme 6 fit | Frame the pitch around sovereignty and privacy; show the threat model and consent/erasure early |
| OCR/VLM errors on handwriting | Incorrect extraction | Confidence gating, mandatory human confirmation |

---

## 14. Assumptions and Open Questions

**Assumptions**
- A workstation with at least one 24 GB+ GPU is available for the demo.
- Public or synthetic datasets (inspection reports, P&IDs) are used; no real confidential data.
- Open-weight model licences permit the intended use.

**Open questions**
1. Which demo domain will be shown to judges: refinery inspection (MRPL) or a neutral domain such as healthcare records?
2. Will Ollama or vLLM be used on demo day (setup speed versus performance)?
3. How deep should the consent and erasure feature go within the time available?
4. Is the aggregate privacy-preserving query (FR-7.7) worth building, or is it better to polish the other demos?

---

## 15. Glossary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Air-gapped | A system with no connection to external networks |
| Egress | Outbound network traffic leaving the system |
| RAG | Retrieval-Augmented Generation, grounding model answers in retrieved documents |
| RBAC | Role-Based Access Control |
| VLM | Vision-Language Model |
| OCR | Optical Character Recognition |
| P&ID | Piping and Instrumentation Diagram |
| Hash chain | A log where each entry includes the hash of the previous entry, making edits detectable |
| Purpose limitation | Using data only for the purpose it was collected or consented for |
